WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:12.680
The term monster derives from the Latin verb
moneo, meaning "to remind, warn, instruct,

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.360
or foretell.”

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:20.990
So it makes sense that the monsters our ancestors
spoke of in hushed tones weren’t just random

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.990
beasts, but reflections of dominant fears
in the cultures that birthed them.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:30.279
In the words of Tina Marie Boyer, professor
at Wake Forest University, "monsters do not

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:35.940
emerge out of a cultural void; they have a
literary and cultural heritage."

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:41.059
And monsters have been part of human culture
since our beginnings.

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:45.320
Incomplete carvings of mythic beasts from
river valley civilizations date back further

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.579
than the first written language.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:53.719
And going even further back, this anthropomorphic
Lion-Man statue, carved out of a mammoth tusk,

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:59.929
might be a depiction of a monster from the
Upper Paleolithic period—about 35 thousand

00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:00.949
years ago.

00:01:00.949 --> 00:01:04.780
Exactly what the statue depicts is debated,
however.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:10.010
Our focus today is instead to identify the
oldest monster explicitly recorded in written

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.010
history.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.400
The creature is likely one you’ve never
heard of.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.010
It comes from the Epic of Gilgamesh, or “He
Who Saw the Unknown” — an epic poem from

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:24.840
ancient Mesopotamia, and quite possibly the
oldest surviving piece of notable literature.Written

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.680
on various clay tablets dating back to 3rd
millennium BCE, the epic chronicles the deeds

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.960
of Gilgamesh, the king of Uruk, and Enkidu,
a wild-man who fights at Gilgamesh’s side.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.960
Throughout the ancient poem, the two warriors
encounter various creatures.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.820
No monster, however, is as powerful or fascinating
as the very first one they meet: Likely the

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.780
first monster in recorded history — Humbaba
The Terrible: No physical descriptions of

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.890
the creature known as Humbaba exist in the
oldest Gilgamesh tablets, but future myths

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.780
depict the being in more detail.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.110
Various records describe his face as a single
coiling line like that of the intestines of

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.190
animals.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.580
Other scattered translations describe him
with “the paws of a lion,” “the claws

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:17.780
of a vulture,” “the horns of a bull,”
and a tail which ends “in a snake’s head.”

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.670
A truly horrific mixture of different features.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.610
Some older carvings of the creature’s face
contain lines which likely represent the coiling

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:32.860
intestines described in the passages, so we
can be pretty certain that that detail, disturbingly,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.730
dates back to the oldest versions of the myth.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.500
And while most ancient carvings of Humbaba
resemble a humanoid, this is likely the result

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.470
of stylization — with the being’s ‘true’
form far more fantastical.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:50.310
So, just how dangerous was the first thing
which goes bump in the night?

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:56.010
Our best account of Humbaba The Terrible being,
well, terrible comes from the second Tablet

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:04.490
of the original Epic – 'This Humbaba, his
voice is the Deluge, his speech is fire, his

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.610
breath is death!

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.650
He hears the forest murmur at sixty leagues’
distance:

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.030
who is there would venture into his forest?”

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.430
Here, ‘The Deluge’ relates to a flood
motif which appears repeatedly in various

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:23.360
cultures, including the later Greek and Norse
mythologies.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.360
Even the great flood mentioned in the Hebrew
Bible was likely influenced by Mesopotamian

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.360
accounts.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:35.760
So, the voice of Humbaba alone is comparable
to an apocalyptic force, which, in many myths

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.180
ends the world.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:42.570
This helps illustrate just how terrifying
the first monster in written history truly

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:43.590
is.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:49.349
Furthermore, in saying ‘His speech is fire,
his breath is death.’

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:51.900
Humbaba almost seems like a force of nature.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.450
A natural disaster given form.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:58.090
And perhaps that’s what he’s intended
to be.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:03.040
Humbaba, like all monsters who followed him,
was an amalgamation of what the society who

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.730
dreamt him up feared.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:11.069
Emerging from a river valley civilization,
Humbaba was likely a personification of humankind’s

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:15.160
fears of floods, wildfires, and other natural
phenomena.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.900
The story of the world’s first monster doesn’t
end there, however.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.550
In Tablet V of the original Epic, Gilgamesh
and Enkidu finally confront Humbaba in his

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.689
home of the Cedar Forest.

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:32.300
According to the tablet, Humbaba is the forest’s
guardian, and kills anyone who enters his

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.370
home.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:37.419
Humbaba then shows he’s capable of speech,
saying to Gilgamesh.

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:44.729
"I will slit the throat and gullet of Gilgamesh,
I will feed his flesh to the locust bird,

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:47.580
ravening eagle and vulture!’”

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.270
The battle begins.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:54.120
The hero-king and the monster trade various
heavy blows, destroying the forest Humbaba

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.530
cares for.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:00.370
In the end, Gilgamesh kills Humbaba by severing
its head with a knife.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:05.279
…But there’s a lot about the account of
Humbaba’s death which seems strange.

00:05:05.279 --> 00:05:11.279
Gilgamesh slays Humbaba with a single dagger
— an unusual end for a supposedly all-powerful

00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:13.169
and murderous monster.

00:05:13.169 --> 00:05:17.419
Furthermore, Gilgamesh isn’t battling the
demon to defend the innocent — he seeks

00:05:17.419 --> 00:05:22.599
out the being in its own home for personal
glory, and acts as the aggressor in challenging

00:05:22.599 --> 00:05:24.289
Humbaba to a fight.

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:28.280
Furthermore, Gilgamesh destroys the forest
Humbaba protected.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.889
It’s seems to go against the classic good
versus evil dynamic we associate with monsters

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:35.029
in ancient myth.

00:05:35.029 --> 00:05:40.849
A more recently uncovered tablet from second
or first millennium BCE offers an alternative

00:05:40.849 --> 00:05:42.719
perspective on the battle.

00:05:42.719 --> 00:05:48.960
In this version Humbaba is described as inhuman,
but a being beloved of the gods and a benevolent

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.449
protector of the forest — and Humbaba, though
powerful, seems at peace and in tune with

00:05:54.449 --> 00:05:57.690
nature before Gilgamesh invades his home.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:03.030
The tablet then portrays Gilgamesh not as
a hero, but as a reckless trespasser who destroys

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.259
the forest without purpose.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:10.590
Humbaba's death is even mourned by Gilgamesh’s
partner Enkidu, as described here: "Enkidu

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:16.400
opened his mouth to speak, saying to Gilgamesh:
'My friend, we have reduced the forest to

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:17.589
a wasteland.

00:06:17.589 --> 00:06:23.050
In your might you slew the guardian, what
was this wrath of yours that you went trampling

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:24.050
the forest?'”

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:30.370
So, is Humbaba, whose voice is the Deluge,
whose speech is fire, and whose breath is

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:34.999
death!, truly the oldest truly monster in
written history, or does that distinction

00:06:34.999 --> 00:06:37.740
perhaps belong to Gilgamesh himself?

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:42.439
Throughout the narrative, Gilgamesh performs
other immoral actions for personal gain.

00:06:42.439 --> 00:06:47.960
In the end, The Epic of Gilgamesh is such
an incomplete and fractured narrative, we’ll

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.879
likely never know for certain who they wanted
audiences to root for.

00:06:51.879 --> 00:06:57.039
But the narrative’s ambiguity suggests The
Ancient Mesopotamians understood both the

00:06:57.039 --> 00:07:02.039
inhuman and the human have the potential to
act monstrous.

00:07:02.039 --> 00:07:05.870
So that’s the tale of the oldest monster
in written history.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:10.740
If you enjoyed this video, please consider
leaving a like, subscribing and pressing the

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.539
notification icon to stay up to date with
Curious Archive.

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:34.330
Thank you so much for watching, and I’ll
see you in the next video.

